          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Leah:</strong> So why is it important to carry out coding reviews?</p>
<p>As with any form of writing, coding involves the use of language and just as with a writer, poet, or dramatist the coders work needs to be ‘proofread’.</p>
<p>When writing code, it is good practice to have someone checking the work that you’re doing as even the most experienced developers can make mistakes or miss something.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> Reviews also significantly ‘improve code quality’, ‘save time’, and can help ‘streamline’ the development process. The results of reviewing can give opportunities for ‘better design’, and suggest more appropriate solutions, or more ‘effective ways of working’, within the project framework.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Code reviews also help with ‘knowledge transfer’ from more experienced developers to those with less experience, enabling them to learn more ‘reliable techniques’ and ‘best practice’. It also helps with the ‘creation of better documentation’ which will be useful when new features or upgrades may be needed.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> Reviewing makes ‘Quality assurance testing easier’. If there are consistent standards in the source code, it makes it easier for specialists or QA testers to understand, which will in turn minimise the need for further testing and reworking.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Code reviews in ‘Agile’ follow the more streamlined approaches such as peer review and peer programming which are less disruptive. Peer reviews are a defining principle of Agile, playing a critical role in creating consistency in the source code, and setting the stage for better ‘QA testing’ and smoother integration.</p>
<p>An important part or the reviewing process is giving feedback this should always be constructive and effective.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> Effective feedback asks questions instead of demanding changes. It gives guidance based on common rules, not just personal opinion and should always ‘add value’. The way you comment on the code is as important as what you are saying, so being kind and respectful goes a long way.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Constructive feedback should be specific and give examples based on documentation and explain reasons for any comments. It is best to avoid repeating comments for the same error and ask for clarification if a segment of code isn’t understood. It is also important to address the team using collective language using ‘we’ instead of ‘you’.</p>
<p>‘Effective feedback’ can reward developers by including positive comments and expressing appreciation for well written code.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> ‘Effective feedback’ will help to improve the quality of code and can help find bugs before the code reaches production. It will also ‘promote sharing knowledge’, increase confidence and trust, align teams, and raise the standard of communication and work within the team. More ‘effective communication’ and ‘improved collaboration’ in the team will help make them more engaged due to a higher level of confidence and trust.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
